From 5ffe31ad7d2183dedf9d9c00f585a1ac8b04e7ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Bansemer <tim.bansemer@inblock.io>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 18:11:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Recover automatically when a call's media key is lost

A member's media key is sent once to each new joiner. In a 1:1 call a key
that does not get through is never sent again, and a failed to-device send
is swallowed and counted as delivered, so the call stays silent until
someone hangs up and redials.

Add src/e2ee/keyRecovery.ts, inside Element Call only:

- wrapKeySend: the first attempt to send a call key is the stock call and
  its outcome reaches the caller at once (js-sdk holds its key distribution
  and rotation on that promise). In the background a rejected send is
  retried after 2 s and 10 s, and a successful one is sent again after 5 s
  and 15 s (up to 8 target devices). Only the latest key of the current join
  is retried or repeated, a newer key drops the pending copies of an older
  one. Other to-device event types pass through untouched.
- SessionGate follows whether our own MatrixRTC session is joined (its
  JoinStateChanged event; never client.clientRunning, which the embedded
  client leaves false). Every join and leave starts a new generation and
  drops all pending sends, timers and episodes of the one before, so
  nothing of an earlier join runs after a hang up or overwrites the key of
  an in-page rejoin.
- KeyRecoveryWatchdog starts on the first decryption error of a remote
  participant. If no new key for that participant arrives within 8 s, it
  refreshes our own call membership state event with a fresh created_ts,
  so every peer sees a new joiner and sends its key again. At most 2
  actions per episode, 15 s apart, and 6 per call. A new key is one with a
  higher index than any installed for that participant, so a late copy of
  an older key does not end the episode. An episode ends on such a key,
  when the participant leaves or the LiveKit connection it was seen on
  closes, and 60 s after its last action.
- The refresh only runs while the session is joined (checked again right
  before the event is sent), in a call of at most 8 members (each refresh
  makes every peer rotate its key), and when our own membership state event
  (looked up by its state key, as MembershipManager writes it) has content.
  A round in which it cannot run, such as sticky event mode, is logged and
  not counted.

Wired in widget.ts (once per client), useLoadGroupCall.ts (the call's
session: join state and received keys) and Connection.ts (encryption
errors, participant gone, connection closed). Tests include the real
RoomWidgetClient with a mocked widget API and the real MatrixRTCSession.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
